{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"semidry-alien-nilgai","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\n`MODEL_LADDER` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/sizing.py:99-103`) is the context-limit escalation\nladder. Two of its three rungs declare the SAME window:\n\n    (\"claude-haiku-4-5\",   200_000),\n    (\"claude-sonnet-4-6\", 1_000_000),\n    (\"claude-opus-4-8\",   1_000_000),\n\nEscalation exists to retry on a LARGER context window after a context-limit failure. A\nsonnet -> opus escalation gains **zero** additional context. The retry that follows is therefore\nguaranteed to hit the same limit and fail again — but at opus pricing, and after a second full\nround-trip.\n\n## Why it is not merely wasteful\n\nThe failure is silent and self-confirming. The escalation reports that it moved to a larger model,\nthe second attempt fails with the same context-limit error, and nothing in the trace says the two\nrungs were the same size. An operator reads it as \"even opus could not fit it\", which is a\ndifferent and more alarming conclusion than the true one: \"we never actually escalated\".\n\nThe wasted spend is the smaller harm. The misleading diagnosis is the larger one.\n\n## Scope — distinct from its neighbours\n\n* **48b3** owns rung LOCATION (which rung the walk starts from for a non-Anthropic primary).\n* **7de4** owns rung TARGET (resolving a rung through the model-class vocabulary).\n* THIS ticket owns the ladder's CONTENT — whether consecutive rungs represent a real capacity\n  increase at all. It is independent of both and can land in any order.\n\n## Approach\n\nDecide what the ladder is for, then make its content honour that. Options to weigh in the plan:\n\n1. Drop the redundant rung, so escalation only ever proposes a strictly larger window.\n2. Keep both rungs but make escalation SKIP any rung whose window is not strictly greater than the\n   current one, so capability differences other than context can still be expressed in the table\n   without producing a no-op retry.\n3. Correct the numbers if either figure is simply wrong — verify both against the providers'\n   published limits before assuming the table is right and the design is wrong.\n\nWhichever is chosen, add a structural guard: consecutive `MODEL_LADDER` entries must have strictly\nincreasing windows, so a future edit cannot reintroduce a no-op rung.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- AC1: A context-limit escalation never proposes a rung whose declared window equals or is smaller\n  than the current model's.\n- AC2: A test asserts `MODEL_LADDER` windows are strictly increasing across consecutive entries,\n  and fails if a no-op rung is added.\n- AC3: The window figures are verified against the providers' documented limits, and the plan states\n  which figure was confirmed and how.\n- AC4: Escalation behaviour for a haiku primary (a rung that IS a real increase) is unchanged,\n  pinned by test.\n- AC5: make lint and make typecheck pass by exit code; the default selection is green.","id":"1157-5b58-b6fa-4710","parent_id":"061c-ecd1-8967-4a76","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"MODEL_LADDER's sonnet and opus rungs share a 1,000,000 window, so context-limit escalation buys nothing at opus pricing"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785688593224598001,"uuid":"e8a7fb34-d629-4809-8a98-a14568f9538a"}